# Does a flow-based intrusion detector survive a change of dataset? A pre-registered study of low-compute domain adaptation on UNSW-NB15 and CIC-IDS-2017

Utsha Basak

*Draft for review. Every number below is generated from the results files in the Prohori
repository; nothing in this text was typed by hand.*

## Abstract

Machine-learning network intrusion detectors are usually evaluated on the dataset they were
trained on, and published scores near the ceiling say little about another network. We measure
the gap on two public flow datasets, UNSW-NB15 and the corrected CIC-IDS-2017, after leakage
controls that remove duplicates, overlap between splits and identifier columns, and with
validation and test data held out in time blocks. A gradient-boosting classifier restricted to
the {{feature_count}} flow features both datasets share reaches a ROC-AUC of {{u2c.common_in_auc}}
and {{c2u.common_in_auc}} within each dataset, but {{u2c.baseline.auc}} (UNSW-NB15 to
CIC-IDS-2017) and {{c2u.baseline.auc}} (the reverse) across them. We then test, under a
pre-registered protocol, six low-compute ways to close that gap without target labels: per-domain
log standardisation, per-domain quantile maps with and without protocol groups, CORAL,
importance weighting from a domain classifier and domain-invariant feature selection, with 10, 50
and 100 labelled target flows as an upper bound. Only the per-domain quantile map improves both
directions ({{u2c.quantile.diff}} and {{c2u.quantile.diff}} ROC-AUC, paired bootstrap intervals
excluding zero and the same sign in every seed). Ten labelled target flows do about as well
({{u2c.k10.auc}} and {{c2u.k10.auc}}), and a hundred do much better ({{u2c.k100.auc}} and
{{c2u.k100.auc}}). A domain classifier separates the two datasets almost perfectly (ROC-AUC
{{u2c.domain_auc}}), which leaves importance weighting nothing to work with. On these two
datasets, labelling a few flows of the target network is worth more than any unsupervised
adaptation we tried.

## 1 Introduction

Flow-based intrusion detection turns each network connection into a vector of statistics
(duration, packet and byte counts, inter-arrival times) and asks a classifier whether it is an
attack. Lab datasets such as UNSW-NB15 [1] and CIC-IDS-2017 [2] made the approach easy to
benchmark, and supervised models routinely score close to perfect on them. Those scores hide
two problems. The first is leakage: duplicated flows, identifiers that encode the attack, and
validation rows that are near-copies of training rows [3, 4, 5, 6]. The second is that a
detector meets a network other than the one it was trained on, a setting machine learning for
security has long been warned about [7, 8].

This paper measures the second problem after controlling the first, and asks whether cheap,
CPU-only methods from the domain-adaptation literature reduce it. The contributions are:

- a cross-dataset evaluation of gradient boosting between UNSW-NB15 and CIC-IDS-2017 on the
  {{feature_count}} features the two datasets share, after leakage controls and time-block
  splits, in both directions and over {{seed_count}} seeds;
- a pre-registered comparison of six unsupervised adaptation methods and a few-shot upper bound,
  with the protocol, settings and decision rule published before any experiment [17];
- diagnostics that explain the negative results, chiefly that the two datasets are almost
  perfectly separable from the shared features;
- complete, byte-reproducible code and results.

## 2 Related work

**Dataset quality.** Engelen, Rimmer and Joosen [3] and Liu et al. [4] documented labelling and
flow-construction errors in CIC-IDS-2017 and released corrected versions, one of which is used
here; Lanvin et al. [5] showed that such errors change detection results materially. Kenyon,
Deka and Elizondo [6] reviewed public intrusion datasets and their fitness for evaluation. Arp
et al. [8] list data snooping and unrealistic evaluation settings among the common pitfalls of
machine learning in security.

**Cross-dataset evaluation.** D'hooge et al. [9] trained twelve supervised algorithms on one
dataset and tested them on another and found that strong within-dataset results do not carry
over; Verkerken et al. [10] reached a similar conclusion for unsupervised detectors. Cantone,
Marrocco and Bria [11] report cross-dataset results close to chance for most combinations of
models and datasets. Layeghy and Portmann [12] evaluated seven models on four datasets, found
none that generalised, and traced the failure with explanation methods to feature-class
relations specific to each dataset. Sarhan, Layeghy and Portmann [13] proposed common NetFlow
feature sets so that datasets can be compared on the same features. Apruzzese, Pajola and Conti
[14] proposed a framework for cross-evaluating intrusion detectors across datasets.

**Domain adaptation.** Covariate-shift correction by importance weighting goes back to
Shimodaira [15]; Bickel, Brückner and Scheffer [16] estimate the weights with a discriminative
domain classifier, the variant used here. CORAL [18] aligns the second-order statistics of the
source with those of the target. Ben-David et al. [19] bound the target error by the source
error plus a divergence between domains, which our domain-classifier diagnostic estimates.
Adversarial methods [20] learn domain-invariant representations but need a deep-learning
framework; they are outside this study's low-compute scope.

This study differs from the cross-dataset work above in three ways: leakage controls and
time-block splits are applied before any cross-dataset number is reported; the adaptation
methods are compared under a protocol fixed in advance; and every arm is reported with paired
bootstrap intervals and a per-seed sign test, so that a method is called helpful only when it
helps in both directions.

## 3 Datasets and leakage controls

**UNSW-NB15** [1] is used through its official training and testing partition; **CIC-IDS-2017**
[2] through the corrected release by Liu et al. [4], in which flows labelled as attempted attacks
carried no attack payload and are treated as benign, as its authors advise. Both datasets are
cleaned the same way before any model sees them: identifier, address, port, time and
label-derived columns are removed; exact duplicates, and every row of a group with identical
features but different classes, are removed; rows of a later split that also occur in an
earlier one are removed; and imputation statistics are learned from training data only.

Validation data (and, for CIC-IDS-2017, test data) are held out as **time blocks**: each
class's flows are put in time order (capture time for CIC-IDS-2017, file order for UNSW-NB15,
whose partition files hold no timestamps) and cut into fifty contiguous blocks, and whole
blocks are held out. A test checks that no training flow lies inside the time range of a
held-out block of its class.

The two datasets were captured with different tools, so the comparison uses the
{{feature_count}} features that can be computed from both: duration, forward and backward
packet and byte counts, mean bytes per packet in each direction, mean inter-arrival time in
each direction, packet and byte rates, and the transport protocol. Byte counts are converted to
bytes on the wire on both sides. After mapping, the datasets are deduplicated again on the
shared features. Table 1 gives the sizes of the splits (seed 0) and the in-dataset ROC-AUC of
the same classifier on all features and on the shared features.

*Table 1. Rows per split after cleaning and mapping (seed 0), and in-dataset ROC-AUC of
gradient boosting (mean over seeds).*

{{table:data}}

Losing features costs little within a dataset; what follows is about what changing dataset
costs.

## 4 Methods

**Protocol.** The questions, the methods with every setting, the metrics, the seeds and the
decision rule were written as an architecture decision record (ADR-0041 in the repository) and
pushed to a public repository before any experiment on the real datasets; the outcome was
appended afterwards without changing the plan. Following Nosek et al. [17], we report every arm
whatever its result.

**Roles of the data.** For each seed and direction, the source's training split is used to fit
the model and its validation split to choose a threshold. The target's training split is an
**unlabelled pool**: its features may be used by the methods allowed to see target data, and
its labels only by the few-shot arms and the reference arm. The target's test split is used for
evaluation only.

**Model.** Histogram gradient boosting from scikit-learn [21] with fixed settings that were never
tuned ({{max_iter}} iterations at most, learning rate {{learning_rate}}, early stopping on a
tenth of the training data), seeded by the run's seed. Thresholds for a {{target_fpr}}
false-positive rate are chosen on the source's benign validation flows and frozen before the
target is scored.

**Arms.** *Baseline*: the shared features, imputed with source medians. *Per-domain log
standardisation*: a signed log transform followed by each domain's own mean and standard
deviation. *Per-domain quantile map*: each feature mapped to its rank within its own domain with
a uniform quantile transform fitted on that domain's unlabelled data; *per protocol*: the same,
fitted separately for TCP, UDP and other traffic. *CORAL* [18]: standardised source features
recoloured with the target covariance. *Importance weighting* [15, 16]: source flows weighted by
the odds that a cross-fitted domain classifier assigns them to the target, clipped and
normalised. *Domain-invariant feature selection*: features whose univariate domain ROC-AUC
reaches a pre-registered cut-off are dropped. *Few-shot*: 10, 50 or 100 labelled flows drawn
from the pool, both classes present, five draws per seed, weighted together like a tenth of the
source. *Reference*: a model trained on the target's own training split, the in-dataset ceiling.
The protocol is never transformed. All settings are in ADR-0041.

**Metrics and statistics.** The primary metric is ROC-AUC on the target's test split, which does
not depend on the share of attacks. Secondary metrics are PR-AUC with its chance level, recall and
false-positive rate at the frozen source threshold, and recall at a {{target_fpr}} false-positive
rate read off the target's own ROC curve (not deployable, but threshold-free). Uncertainty comes
from {{resamples}} bootstrap resamples [22] of the target test flows per seed, shared by every arm
so that differences are paired, averaged over the seeds. An arm **improves** a direction only if
the {{ci_level}} interval of its ROC-AUC difference to the baseline excludes zero and the difference has
the same sign in every seed (the bootstrap covers evaluation noise, the sign test training
variance); it **helps** only if it improves both directions.

## 5 Results

**The harness reproduces the earlier evaluation.** In every seed and direction the baseline and
the reference arm match the repository's separate cross-dataset results exactly.

*Table 2. UNSW-NB15 → CIC-IDS-2017: target-test results, means over seeds {{seeds}}.*

{{table:u2c}}

*Table 3. CIC-IDS-2017 → UNSW-NB15: target-test results, means over seeds {{seeds}}.*

{{table:c2u}}

![ROC-AUC difference to the baseline per arm, with {{ci_level}} bootstrap intervals; few-shot arms in brown.](figures/fig1-roc-auc-difference.svg)

**The gap is large in both directions.** Trained on UNSW-NB15, the classifier scores
{{u2c.baseline.auc}} on CIC-IDS-2017 against a ceiling of {{u2c.reference.auc}}; trained on
CIC-IDS-2017 it scores {{c2u.baseline.auc}} on UNSW-NB15 against {{c2u.reference.auc}}. Its
frozen threshold flags {{u2c.baseline.fpr_src}} and {{c2u.baseline.fpr_src}} of the target's
benign flows instead of {{target_fpr}}.

**One unsupervised method helps.** The per-domain quantile map raises ROC-AUC to
{{u2c.quantile.auc}} ({{u2c.quantile.diff}}, interval {{u2c.quantile.diff_ci}}) and
{{c2u.quantile.auc}} ({{c2u.quantile.diff}}, interval {{c2u.quantile.diff_ci}}), closing
{{u2c.quantile.gap}} and {{c2u.quantile.gap}} of the gap. Even so its frozen threshold flags
{{u2c.quantile.fpr_src}} and {{c2u.quantile.fpr_src}} of benign target flows: better ranking does
not give a usable operating point. Methods that help in both directions: {{helping_unsupervised}}.

**The others do not.** Importance weighting improves one direction ({{c2u.importance.diff}}) and
worsens the other ({{u2c.importance.diff}}). CORAL lowers ROC-AUC to {{u2c.coral.auc}} from
UNSW-NB15 to CIC-IDS-2017. Per-domain log standardisation changes the result in opposite
directions from seed to seed. The per-protocol quantile map, expected to refine the plain one,
worsens both directions ({{u2c.qprotocol.diff}} and {{c2u.qprotocol.diff}}). Feature selection
dropped no feature, so that arm equals the baseline.

**A few labels beat every unsupervised method.** Figure 2 shows target ROC-AUC against the
number of labelled target flows. Ten flows reach {{u2c.k10.auc}} and {{c2u.k10.auc}}, about the
quantile map's level; a hundred reach {{u2c.k100.auc}} and {{c2u.k100.auc}}, closing
{{u2c.k100.gap}} and {{c2u.k100.gap}} of the gap. Arms that help in both directions:
{{helping}}.

![Target ROC-AUC against the number of labelled target flows, with the quantile map (no labels) and the target-trained reference.](figures/fig2-few-shot.svg)

**Diagnostics.** Table 4 explains the failures. A cross-fitted domain classifier separates the
datasets almost perfectly from the shared features (ROC-AUC {{u2c.domain_auc}} and
{{c2u.domain_auc}}), although no single feature does: each feature's univariate domain ROC-AUC
lies between {{feature_auc_min}} and {{feature_auc_max}}. The datasets differ in how features
combine, not in any one of them, so selecting features by their individual shift removes nothing,
and importance weights pile up at their clips, leaving an effective sample of {{u2c.ess}} and
{{c2u.ess}} of the source.

*Table 4. Diagnostics, means over seeds.*

{{table:diagnostics}}

## 6 Discussion

The negative results are informative. Covariate-shift weighting assumes that source and target
share support; here a classifier tells them apart from the shared features almost without error,
so there is no overlap to reweight, consistent with the divergence term in the bound of
Ben-David et al. [19]. CORAL aligns second-order statistics, but the distributions differ in
shape and in how features relate, which a linear recolouring cannot fix and can make worse. The
quantile map aligns each feature's marginal distribution and helps most when the two tools
measure the same quantity on different scales; it cannot repair relations between features, and
it aligns the attack mix along with benign traffic (see Section 7). Grouping by protocol thins
the data the maps are fitted on and removes the alignment of protocol mix, which may explain why
it is worse than the plain map.

For practice, the result is simple: on these two datasets, a model must be adapted with labels
from the network it will watch. A hundred labelled flows did more
than any unsupervised method, and the in-dataset ceiling shows how much more labels can buy.

## 7 Threats to validity

- **Shared features.** Only {{feature_count}} features can be computed from both datasets, and
  they were measured by different tools that also cut flows differently. This limits everything,
  and a richer common representation might transfer better.
- **The unlabelled pool is not real traffic.** The pools are lab training splits with
  {{u2c.pool_attack}} (CIC-IDS-2017) and {{c2u.pool_attack}} (UNSW-NB15) attacks; real unlabelled
  traffic is mostly benign. Per-domain transforms and the domain classifier therefore align the
  attack mix as well as the benign traffic (label shift), and the quantile map's gain may be
  smaller on a real network.
- **A small label leak in the pool.** Cleaning removed rows with identical features but different
  classes, using labels; every arm shares it.
- **Scope.** Two datasets, one pair, {{seed_count}} seeds and one model family. A method that
  fails here may work elsewhere, and the one that helps here may not.
- **Lab data.** Both datasets are years old and synthetic in their background traffic.

## 8 Limitations and future work

Adversarial adaptation [20] and other neural methods were not run (they need a deep-learning
framework, outside the study's CPU-only scope); a third dataset, which would show whether the
quantile map's gain generalises to another pair, was not added. Hyper-parameters were never
tuned, by design. The few-shot arms use flows drawn at random; choosing which flows to label
(active learning) is a natural next step.

## 9 Reproducibility statement

The code, results and this text are in the Prohori repository (version {{prohori_version}}); the
study used scikit-learn {{sklearn}}, numpy {{numpy}} and pandas {{pandas}}, with every package
version pinned in the repository's lockfile. With the two datasets downloaded as the repository's
`docs/DATASETS.md` describes:

```bash
uv run python -m prohori.ml.train --dataset unsw-nb15 --seeds 0 1 2
uv run python -m prohori.ml.train --dataset cicids2017 --seeds 0 1 2
uv run python -m prohori.ml.transfer --seeds 0 1 2
uv run python scripts/cross_dataset_study.py
uv run python scripts/build_paper.py
```

Seeds {{seeds}} drive every split, model and resample; a second run of the study gave a
byte-identical results file on the same platform (linear-algebra results may differ in the last
digits on another). The protocol is ADR-0041 in `docs/DECISIONS.md`. Prohori is source-available
under the PolyForm Strict License 1.0.0: readers may run the study for non-commercial research,
but may not change or redistribute the code without the author's permission. The datasets are
not redistributed and keep their publishers' terms.

